/*! \page developer_backend_id_page Backend Identifier Classes

\ref developer_backend_page       (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_field_page (Next)    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

PSCF uses C++ templates to define closely analogous classes that use 
different computational backends. All classes and functions that must 
be specialized to allow the use of more than one possible backend are 
defined as templates that accept a special type of template parameter
that is used to identify a choice of computational backend.
that we refer to as a template identifier class.

PSCF defines two classes, named Pscf:CPT and Pscf::CUT, that we refer to
as template identifier classes.  These classes are designed specifically
to be used as template parameter arguments in order to specify a choice 
of computational backend:
  - Use of the class Pscf::CPT as a template argument indicates a template 
    specialization that uses only CPU hardware, and that contains only 
    standard C++ code.
  - Use of the class Pscf::CUT as a template argument indicates a template 
    specialization that uses GPU hardware, and that relies on some code
    that is programmed in CUDA C++.

A template parameter that is designed to take one of these backend 
identifier classes as its argument is referredt to a a backend identifier 
template parameter, or simply a backend identifier parameter  By convention, 
backend identifier parameters are always denoted by the symbol "T" in 
template definitions.

Many of the class templates that accept a backend identifier template
parameter also take the integer dimension of spaced, denoted by D, as an 
additional parameter parameter. In such templates, the int parameter D 
is always listed as the first template parameter, and a backend template 
parameter T as a second.  Almost all of the class templates defined in 
namespace Pscf::Rp are templates with these two template parameters.

For example, the Pscf::Rp::System class template, which represents the 
highest level object in a SCFT or PS-SCFT calculation, is declared 
within the Pscf::Rp namespace by a template with an signature
\code
    template <int D, class T> class System;
\endcode
An instantiation of this template must use an integer argument D=1, 2, 
or 3 for the int parameter D, and one of the template identifier classes 
as the argument for the class parameter T.  In code that is defined 
within the Pscf::Rp namespace, System \<3, CUT\> is thus the name of
a specialization of the System template that uses a CUDA GPU backend 
to simulate a physical model that uses 3-dimensional periodic fields. 

<BR>
\ref developer_backend_page       (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_field_page (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;

*/
